  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 138 AM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThrough this weekend, prevailing flow will be out of the north as a \ndeveloping nor'easter in the New England region stalls. The trough \naxis associated with this system will extend southwards to the \nwestern Atlantic waters off the Florida coast. Thus, South Florida\nwill remain in northerly flow pattern through the weekend and \nthis will keep drier air in the atmospheric column along with \ninhibition of deep convection. While some positive vorticity \nimpulses will stream across the area and the diurnal sea breezes \nwill still form, these along may not be enough to overcome the \ninhibition. Nevertheless, some air parcels will be capable of \nlifting high enough to produce some isolated to scattered showers \nor storms but they will not be overly strong. In general, \nexpecting convection to be of the weaker variety through Sunday.\n\nBy early next week, the system in the northeast US will shift more \nnorthwards as a mid-to-upper level shortwave trough pushes across\nthe central US and deepens across the Mid-Atlantic and Southeast \nUS regions. As this trough swings through the Florida Peninsula, \nlow level flow will shift back to a southeasterly direction and \nresult in the return of tropical moisture advection. With deeper \nmoisture arriving and this trough providing some upper level \nforcing for ascent, rain chances will increase back to the 60-80% \nrange by Tuesday and into the middle of next week. Any potential \nimpacts will be assessed later on since this is at the end of the \nforecast period and uncertainty is high. \n\nHigh temperatures each day are still expected in the upper 80s to \naround 90 each day for most areas. Overnight lows will be a few \ndegrees cooler with the front clearing the area, with lows near the \nlake region in the mid 60s and the rest of the region seeing lows in \nthe upper 60s to low 70s.",
